# Netlify routing rules. Order matters — the FIRST matching rule wins, so the
# API proxy must come before the SPA catch-all or every /api/* request gets
# answered with index.html.
#
# ── Why the proxy is here ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The client calls the API at a relative `/api` path (VITE_API_URL defaults to
# '/api' — see src/api/client.ts). That works on Render's static hosting because
# render.yaml declares an /api/* rewrite to the backend service. Netlify has no
# such rule unless it is declared here.
#
# Without this file's first line, every API request goes to
# https://app.quantiqsystems.com/api/... , matches the SPA catch-all below, and
# comes back as the index.html page with HTTP 200. Axios then chokes on HTML
# where it expected JSON, and *every* call in the app fails at once — login
# included. That is the failure this file exists to prevent.
#
# ── Why proxy rather than set VITE_API_URL to the backend origin ─────────────
# A same-origin proxy keeps the browser talking to one domain, so there is no
# CORS preflight to configure, no cross-site cookie behaviour to reason about,
# and Server-Sent Events (src/hooks/useSSE.ts) keep working — EventSource cannot
# send an Authorization header cross-origin. Pointing VITE_API_URL at
# https://crm-itdesk-server.onrender.com/api also works, but then CORS_ORIGIN on
# the backend must list this exact domain, and the SSE path needs its own fix.
#
# If the backend ever moves, change the target below and redeploy the frontend.

/api/*  https://crm-itdesk-server.onrender.com/api/:splat  200!

# SPA fallback — every non-asset, non-API route serves the app shell.
/*      /index.html                                        200
